  "query_preview": "..\n\nPrimary f-gen responsible for last evening's moderate to, at times, \nheavy rain has largely shifted west and north of the area overnight. \nAside from the northern CWA who still reside under the edge of this \ndeformation axis, the rest of the area has seen an expansive dry \nslot stretch in from the Appalachians resulting in an end to \nwidespread showers instead transitioning to pockets of drizzle/light \nshowers. Arrival of surface low pressure over Lake Erie and the \nparent mid-upper low into the eastern Ohio valley brings a renewed \nperiod of ascent/low level moisture supporting the expansion of \nscattered to numerous showers this morning. While rainy weather \ncontinues, column winds decrease through the morning as the LLJ \ndriving yesterday's gusty conditions weakens and shifts \nnortheastward following the low track. This allows peak gusts to \nfall more in the 20-25mph range while winds back to the northwest \nover the course of the day. There is a window in the afternoon for \nsignificant drop in shower coverage, the result of low pressure \nreorganizing over the eastern Great Lakes, turning nearly vertically \nstacked, and inducing slight height rises locally. That said, \ntrailing deformation axis that had departed for western MI this \nmorning is drawn back east with the departing trough maintaining at \nleast scattered light showers over SE MI through the evening. \n\nAforementioned height rises continue to build into lower MI tapering \noff rain by late evening. Shortwave ridging quickly slides over \nlower MI tonight stripping out remnant moisture as PW falls to \naround 0.25\". This is expected to keep a dry Saturday for the bulk \nof the region despite a weak mid-level wave and accompanying \nelevated warm front lifting across lower MI. Saginaw Valley stands \nthe best chance at seeing a few light showers daytime Saturday, \nowing to proximity to the wave. Very modest warm advection occurs \nfollowing the front as highs peak in the mid 40s, a couple degrees \nabove average.\n\nQuiet period is shortlived as a Pacific NW originating trough \narrives over the Midwest late Saturday night spurring cyclogensis \nnear the confluence of the Mississippi-Ohio rivers. New surface low \npressure rapidly lifts northeast across OH daytime Sunday with the \nlead arm of isentropic ascent reaching SE MI by afternoon. This \ntrack places the CWA favorably under the northwestern flank \ndeformation axis supporting persistent precip through the first half \nof Sunday night. Despite residing on the cool side of the low, \ncolder airmass can't quite make the connection in time only reaching \nWI by evening. Result is thermal profiles with wet bulb zero heights \n3-4kft agl supporting p-types as a cold rain. Some snow could mix in \nat the very end of precipitation Sunday night as cold air (<0C 925mb \ntemps) finally arrive, however rapid departure of the low makes this \nwindow extremely narrow. Increasing mixing depths within this cold \nadvection likely support gustier northwesterly winds (25-30mph) late \nSunday night through the first part of Monday keeping wind chills in \nthe lower 20s. \n\nTX originating high pressure builds into the central Great Lakes \nlatter half of Monday rapidly diminishing winds as well as veering \nflow back toward the southwest by Tuesday. Amplifying upper ridging \nover the central CONUS midweek further moderates temperatures with \nlonger range models advertising a return to much above normal \ntemperatures.",
